WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Flow matching turns random noise into shapes.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.433
On the left is noise: random points from a bell-shaped
distribution.

3
00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:11.767
On the right is a branching, neuron-like shape, made of
points.

4
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:15.733
Both live in the same plane, so we overlay them.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:20.800
Join each noise point to a random shape point with a
straight line.

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.967
As time t runs from 0 to 1, each point slides from noise
to the shape.

7
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:32.033
Freeze at t = 0.5. Each point moves at a steady
velocity.

8
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:36.367
Take one pair: noise point x₀ and shape point y.

9
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:43.967
It travels from x₀ to y in one unit of time, so its
velocity is y − x₀.

10
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:49.767
Training teaches a neural network to predict this
velocity from the position and the time.

11
00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:56.267
Many paths pass each spot at the same time, so it can
only learn their average velocity.

12
00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:03.867
Here we can compute that average velocity exactly, at
every spot. A real model trains a network to approximate
it.

13
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:09.467
To generate, start from fresh noise and take small steps
along the field.

14
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:13.067
After 40 steps, the noise has become the shape.

15
00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:19.233
This exact field only rebuilds these points. A trained
network's smoother field can make new shapes.

16
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:25.733
One step lands every point on the average. Three steps
blur the shape. Forty make it sharp.

17
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:33.033
The same idea works for 3D point clouds, with three
coordinates per point.

18
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:38.467
Learn the velocity that carries noise to data. Then
follow it in small steps.
